<h2>The case against the officials</h2>
<p>FIR No. 07/2026, registered on 2 June 2026 by the Anti-Corruption Branch of the Delhi Government, alleged a procurement cartel: specifications in DGHS and Central Procurement Agency tenders drafted to fit the products of firms linked to a co-accused, with payments of about Rs 842 crore flowing to five companies. Dr. Ranga had served as Head of Office of the CPA; Dr. Vatsala Aggarwal, the second applicant, as Director General of Health Services. The chargesheet was filed on 16 September 2026, cognizance was taken by the Special Judge (PC Act) at Rouse Avenue, and further investigation under the BNSS continued.</p>

<h2>The rival contentions</h2>
<div class="compare">
  <div class="col old">
    <strong>The State&rsquo;s opposition.</strong> A grave economic offence of Rs 842 crore; tender files missing and never produced; phones not produced or passwords withheld; further investigation at a crucial stage; a real risk of evidence-tampering and witness influence, given the applicants&rsquo; seniority. Reliance was placed on the Supreme Court&rsquo;s economic-offence bail decisions.
  </div>
  <div class="arrow">→</div>
  <div class="col new">
    <strong>The applicants&rsquo; case.</strong> Investigation against them complete — chargesheet, sanction and cognizance all in place; no custodial interrogation sought since mid-August; the State&rsquo;s own reply showed no transfer into Dr. Ranga&rsquo;s personal account; 84 witnesses mean a long trial; Dr. Aggarwal was not named in the FIR, had no custody of the tender files, is 61 and unwell — attracting the proviso to Section 480(1) BNSS for women and sick accused.
  </div>
</div>

<h2>The court&rsquo;s reasoning</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Necessity, not gravity.</strong> The anchor of the order: &ldquo;the refusal of bail must rest on a demonstrable necessity to keep the accused in custody.&rdquo; Gravity frames the inquiry; it does not answer it.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>A documents case, already seized.</strong> The prosecution&rsquo;s material is documentary and electronic, and it is in the agency&rsquo;s hands. Where the evidence is secured, the tampering argument loses most of its force.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Custody cannot compel production.</strong> The tender files went missing before the arrests. &ldquo;Whether the explanation of loss is true, and what inference would follow if it is not, are matters for trial&rdquo; — detention is not a lever to extract documents, and call records are obtainable from service providers in any event.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>No pre-trial punishment.</strong> With no money trail to either applicant, no antecedents and no flight risk, continued detention would serve no investigative purpose: &ldquo;At this stage, the bail cannot be withheld as a form of pre-trial punishment.&rdquo; The Supreme Court&rsquo;s economic-offence precedents pressed by the State were distinguished on their facts — those were cases of time-bound, ongoing investigations or altogether different offences.</div>
</div>

<h2>What the order signals</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><strong>Post-chargesheet custody needs fresh justification.</strong> Once the chargesheet is filed and cognizance taken, the investigative rationale for custody thins. Agencies that neither seek custodial interrogation nor identify concrete pending steps will struggle to resist bail.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>The statutory proviso has teeth.</strong> The proviso to Section 480(1) BNSS — the legislative policy favouring bail for women, the sick and the infirm — was expressly weighed for the second applicant alongside the merits.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Conditions do the work of custody.</strong> Passport surrender, premises and contact bans, and monthly reporting address the pleaded risks directly. The modern bail order manages risk; it does not warehouse the accused.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Economic-offence rhetoric has limits.</strong> Describing an offence as economic and grave does not displace the bail framework. The distinguishing exercise in this order shows how fact-specific the leading precedents really are.</div>
</div>

<div class="note">The findings are prima facie and confined to bail; the trial on the Rs 842 crore allegations will proceed on its own evidence. For practitioners, the order is a useful template on both sides: the defence framing of a document-based, already-seized case, and the prosecution&rsquo;s burden to show what custody would actually achieve that conditions cannot.</div>
